<!--
    GH-6882: SPARC struct-return `unimp N` after a call mis-handled.
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/6882

    The SPARC ABI for a function returning a structure has the callee return
    to caller+12 (not caller+8), and the compiler plants an `unimp <structsize>`
    instruction in the gap (caller+8) as a struct-size hint.  Per the SPARC
    SLEIGH spec (SparcV9.sinc), `unimp`/`illtrap` lifts to

        dest = IllegalInstructionTrap(N);
        goto [dest];                       # "trap should not fall thru by default"

    i.e. a CALLOTHER feeding a BRANCHIND.  Jump-table recovery cannot resolve
    the indirect target, so flow.cc::truncateIndirectJump rewrites the BRANCHIND
    into a non-returning CALLIND ("Does not return") and the C renders a bogus
    indirect call `(*pcVar1)()`; and, because the trap is treated as a
    terminator, the rest of the function (the second call, the return) is lost.

    Two functions are present.  `f` @0x0 is a struct-return function whose body
    ends with the struct-return tail `jmp %i7+12 ; restore` (SparcV9.sinc line
    1026 already lifts this to `return`).  `p` @0x10 calls `f`, and the compiler
    planted `unimp 0x8` right after the call (image 0x30); `p` then makes a
    second call and returns normally.

    Stage model: S2 "flow-classification" sub-stage (call-vs-branch / terminator
    classification).  The mis-classification of the post-call `unimp` BRANCHIND
    as a non-returning indirect call starves the rest of the function.

    Fix surface = a default-off ArchOption (sparcstructret) that, at flow time
    (FlowInfo::xrefControlFlow), recognises the SPARC struct-return idiom (a
    BRANCHIND whose target is the result of an IllegalInstructionTrap CALLOTHER
    in the same instruction) and makes it fall through instead of becoming a
    non-returning CALLIND, so the rest of the function is recovered.

    Pass 1 (default, option off): assertion #1 proves the bug reproduces; the
    post-call `unimp` renders as a computed call to a function pointer
    `(*pcVar1)()` and the function tail is lost.
    Pass 2 (option sparcstructret on): assertion #2 proves the fix; the `unimp`
    falls through, the rest of the function is recovered, and `p` renders its
    real return tail (the bogus `(*pcVar1)()` is gone).  The two assertions are
    over the cumulative two-pass output: `(*pcVar1)()` appears exactly once
    (pass 1 only) and the recovered tail exactly once (pass 2 only).

    NOTE on assertion #2's text, revision 1: it used to look for `return CONCAT44`,
    because the recovered tail arrived as a spurious o0:o1 join, the GH-6990
    artifact: `p` returns normally and has no struct return.
    `kuna_returnuncomputed` now drops that passive half by default, so the tail
    rendered as `return v1 + a0`.  The assertion names the expression directly: it
    tests the thing the option is for (the tail exists at all) rather than a
    symptom of a second, unrelated bug.

    NOTE on assertion #2's text, revision 2 (callsitestackargs): the expected text
    is now `return v1[0] + a0`.  THE OLD TEXT WAS WRONG, and this is not a
    re-record of a regression.  `f` is a struct-return function, so its caller
    passes a hidden pointer to an 8-byte return buffer.  kuna was dropping that
    pointer along with every other stack-passed call argument (the mis-ported
    localrange probe in check_input_trial_use), so `p` rendered `f()` with no
    arguments at all and the buffer degenerated to a scalar `int4 v1`.  With
    `callsitestackargs` on (the default) the pointer is passed, `f(v2)` where
    `v2 = v1`, the buffer types correctly as `int4 v1 [2]`, and the tail reads its
    first word: `return v1[0] + a0`.  Both halves of the output got MORE correct;
    only the pinned expression text had to follow.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x0">
